@node Speech Backends
@chapter Speech Backends

Emacsvox needs a speech-server process.  For a new installation, use
@strong{Omnivox}.  Omnivox is the server that Emacsvox talks to; the engine
that produces speech underneath it is platform-specific.  The normal engine
is eSpeak NG on GNU/Linux, AVSpeechSynthesizer on macOS, and WinRT
SpeechSynthesizer on Windows.

This chapter owns Emacsvox's platform recommendation and first backend smoke
test.  Omnivox owns its build internals, engine behavior, and versioned
protocols.  The maintained Omnivox source and current status are at
@url{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox, the Omnivox repository}.

@menu
* Supported Platform Routes::  Choose the maintained route for this machine.
* Installing Omnivox From Source::  Install on GNU/Linux or macOS.
* Building Omnivox For WSL2::  Stage the Windows runtime from WSL2.
* Selecting And Checking Omnivox::  Prove speech before starting Emacsvox.
* Compatibility Speech Servers::  Understand the retained direct adapters.
* Backend Diagnostics And Ownership::  Locate failures and authoritative detail.
@end menu

@node Supported Platform Routes
@section Supported Platform Routes

The following table describes Emacsvox support, not every platform that the
Omnivox project can build.  ``Host check required'' means that the source path
is maintained but an audible @command{omnivox --check} on the user's machine
is still the acceptance test.

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .31 .51
@headitem Emacs platform @tab Recommended route @tab Current support evidence
@item GNU/Linux
@tab Source-built Omnivox using eSpeak NG
@tab Supported source installation; host check required.  Omnivox does not
currently publish a Linux archive or run a Linux audio-runtime CI job.
@item macOS on Apple silicon
@tab Source-built Omnivox using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Supported source installation; Omnivox CI executes this native target,
and the host check remains required.
@item macOS on Intel
@tab Source-built Omnivox using AVSpeechSynthesizer
@tab Available source installation; current Omnivox CI cross-builds this
target but does not execute it.  Treat the host check as required validation.
@item Emacs in WSL2 on x86-64 Windows
@tab Emacsvox-staged Windows Omnivox using WinRT
@tab Supported advanced deployment; the pinned build verifies its payload,
but speech and audio must be accepted on the target Windows host.
@item Native Windows Emacs
@tab Omnivox executable on @code{exec-path}, using WinRT
@tab Preview.  The executable-resolution path exists, but Emacsvox does not
yet publish or fresh-machine test a complete native-Windows setup.  Initial
users should use the WSL2 route.
@end multitable

There is no verified public binary-download path in this guide.  Build from
source as described below.  A repository tag, a CI artifact, or an executable
without its adjacent runtime data is not a substitute for a supported install
payload.

Do not set @env{OMNIVOX_ENGINE} during initial setup.  Its empty value selects
the normal platform engine above.  Engine routing and optional Piper,
Eloquence, or DECtalk support are later customizations, not prerequisites for
first speech.

@node Installing Omnivox From Source
@section Installing Omnivox From Source

Use this route on GNU/Linux and macOS.  It requires network access for the
initial clone, Rust toolchain, and locked dependency downloads.

Install these prerequisite classes before building:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@url{https://rustup.rs/, rustup}; the checked-in
@file{rust-toolchain.toml} selects the supported Rust release.
@item
Python 3, a C and C++ toolchain, CMake, and Clang with libclang.  The supported
build wrapper uses these to build and stage eSpeak NG and its generated data.
@item
On GNU/Linux, @command{pkg-config} and ALSA development headers.  The ALSA
package is commonly named @code{libasound2-dev} on Debian or Ubuntu and
@code{alsa-lib-devel} on Fedora.
@item
On macOS, the Xcode command-line tools.  Install them with
@command{xcode-select --install} if they are absent.
@end itemize

Keep the two repositories beside one another; this also matches the WSL2
release layout:

@example
cd /path/containing/emacsvox
git clone https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox
cd omnivox
make build
make install
@end example

@command{make build} performs the locked release build and stages the matching
eSpeak data and third-party notices.  @command{make install} installs the
binary and those adjacent directories, normally below @file{~/.cargo/bin}.
Set @env{OMNIVOX_INSTALL_BIN} before @command{make install} only when the Cargo
binary directory is elsewhere.  Do not use a bare @command{cargo build} as the
installation procedure; it does not create the complete staged payload.

Make the install directory visible on @env{PATH} before continuing.  No
proprietary speech runtime is needed for the recommended Linux or macOS path.

@node Building Omnivox For WSL2
@section Building Omnivox For WSL2

Use this route when Emacs runs inside WSL2 and speech should play through
Windows.  Clone Omnivox beside Emacsvox as shown above.  The host must provide:

@itemize @bullet
@item
WSL2 with working @command{powershell.exe} and @command{wslpath} interoperation;
@item
Docker with Linux/AMD64 container support;
@item
Windows .NET Framework 4.7.2 or newer; and
@item
@command{curl}, @command{unzip}, and @command{sha256sum} in WSL, plus network
access for the pinned container and .NET toolchain inputs.
@end itemize

From a clean Emacsvox checkout whose sibling Omnivox checkout also has no
tracked changes, run:

@example
cd /path/to/emacsvox
make windows-omnivox
@end example

This is the reproducible release path.  It builds and verifies a pinned
Windows-GNU executable and stages a content-addressed runtime beneath
@file{servers/omnivox-bin}.  It also copies the runnable payload to a
Windows-local directory so native voice and eSpeak data discovery do not pay
the WSL filesystem cost.  Optional proprietary Eloquence and DECtalk runtimes
are not downloaded or distributed.

Maintainers testing tracked changes may use
@command{make windows-omnivox-dev}.  That target records both worktree-diff
hashes and labels the result as a development build; it is not the clean
release procedure.  See @file{servers/omnivox-release/README.org} for the
pinned toolchain, licensing, provenance, and restaging contract.

@node Selecting And Checking Omnivox
@section Selecting And Checking Omnivox

First test Omnivox without Emacs.  On GNU/Linux or macOS, run the installed
binary:

@example
omnivox --version
omnivox --check
omnivox --list-voices
@end example

Under WSL2, test the content-addressed launcher instead:

@example
./servers/omnivox --check
@end example

The check initializes the selected engine and audio output, then plays a tone
and speech.  Success ends with a message saying that everything is working if
both were heard.  Do not continue merely because the command exited zero:
hearing both signals is the backend acceptance condition.

Next select the server before loading Emacsvox:

@example
export TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox
@end example

Make that setting visible in the shell used to launch Emacs.  An
@command{export} affects only that shell and its children; placing it in a
shell startup file makes the choice persistent for later sessions.  On POSIX
systems, Emacsvox's @file{servers/omnivox} launcher finds an installed
@command{omnivox} on @env{PATH}; @env{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can instead name its
absolute executable.  Native Windows Emacs resolves @command{omnivox.exe}
directly through @code{exec-path}, but that route remains preview.

Inside a running Emacsvox session, @code{M-x omnivox} selects the same server
temporarily.  Use @code{M-x tts-speak-version} to identify the live adapter
and @code{M-x tts-restart} after changing server configuration.  This backend
contract stops at a proven speech server; the complete isolated Emacs start,
expected startup speech, and no-speech recovery belong to the first-use
procedure.

@node Compatibility Speech Servers
@section Compatibility Speech Servers

Emacsvox retains direct speech servers for existing installations.  They are
not the recommended first setup and are outside the initial-user platform
matrix above:

@table @code
@item espeak
The direct GNU/Linux Tcl server.  Building it with @command{make espeak}
requires Tclx at runtime, Tcl 8.6 development headers, eSpeak NG development
libraries, and a C++ compiler.  It remains useful for compatibility testing,
but new users should install Omnivox instead.
@item swiftmac
The direct Swift macOS server.  It requires Swift 5.9 or newer and is built
with @command{make swiftmac}.  It remains a compatibility alternative to
Omnivox's AVSpeechSynthesizer engine; see
@file{servers/mac-swiftmac/Readme.org}.
@item mac
The legacy Python/PyObjC macOS server.  The unset runtime selector still names
it as a compatibility default on Darwin, but it is not the recommended or
documented initial-user route.  It depends on Foundation, AppKit, and
PyObjCTools availability.
@item outloud
The direct GNU/Linux IBM TTS or Eloquence adapter.  It requires a separately
licensed compatible runtime, which Emacsvox does not distribute.  Voxin
availability is documented at @url{https://voxin.oralux.net}.
@item dtk-soft
The direct Software DECtalk adapter.  Install its runtime separately and see
@file{servers/software-dtk/Readme.org}.  Emacsvox does not distribute licensed
speech runtimes.
@end table

The native Windows audio, standalone Eloquence, and standalone DECtalk bridges
under @file{servers/} are advanced WSL-specific compatibility paths.  Piper is
an optional Omnivox engine and model workflow, not a primary Emacsvox server.

@node Backend Diagnostics And Ownership
@section Backend Diagnostics And Ownership

Use the boundary at which speech failed to choose the next diagnostic:

@table @asis
@item @command{omnivox} is not found
Check @env{PATH}; on POSIX, an absolute @env{OMNIVOX_PROGRAM} can identify the
installed executable.  Keep its adjacent @file{espeak-ng-data} and
@file{third-party-licenses} directories with it.
@item @command{omnivox --check} reports an error or is inaudible
The failure is below Emacsvox.  Consult Omnivox's
@url{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/README.md, build guide},
@url{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/STATUS.md, platform status},
and
@url{https://github.com/bartbunting/omnivox/blob/main/docs/DIAGNOSTICS.md,
diagnostics guide}.  Do not diagnose Emacs configuration until the tone and
speech check passes.
@item The check passes but Emacsvox is silent
Confirm that @env{TTS_PROGRAM=omnivox} was present before Emacsvox loaded, then
use @code{M-x tts-speak-version}.  Under WSL2, also run
@command{make verify-windows-omnivox-runtime} and restart the speech process
after restaging.
@item A process starts and later fails
The Emacsvox POSIX/WSL launcher writes private, bounded stderr logs below
@file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/emacsvox/omnivox}, defaulting to
@file{~/.local/state/emacsvox/omnivox}.  Spoken text is excluded unless
@env{OMNIVOX_LOG_SYNTHESIS_TEXT} is explicitly enabled; enabling it can expose
passwords, messages, and document contents.
@end table

Emacsvox owns server selection, its POSIX/WSL launcher, Windows staging, and
the bundled Lisp adapter.  Omnivox owns its native builds, engines, command
line, runtime data layout, and protocol specifications.  Backend-local
READMEs own licensed-runtime and compatibility-adapter details.  Cross-link
these owners rather than copying their versioned contracts into installation
instructions.
